// A Read is permitted if there was formerly a Login. The policy predicate
// mentions only `input.user` — it never writes the session id. The event
// schema PINS the NESTED leaf `__drupe.session_id` to
// `context.__drupe.session_id` (see event.dwschema), so the pin forces
// the past Login to share the current Read's session. Proves pins work on a
// nested (dotted) field path, and cannot be bypassed by a directly-written
// predicate.
//
// Positive/negative control across two traces:
//   trace_1: prior Login shares the Read's session id → permitted.
//   trace_2: IDENTICAL except the prior Login's session id differs → the
//            injected nested pin forces a mismatch → NOT permitted.

permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: context.input.user }
};
